# Testing

- Keep tests under `tests/`; never ship test-only dependencies in production projects.
- Prefer deterministic unit tests; mark lab/integration cases skippable when external systems are unavailable.
- Do not weaken assertions or raise arbitrary timeouts to hide races—fix the race or wait for the real invariant.
- Do not disable parallelization globally without evidence of shared mutable state.
- Report exact pass/fail/skip counts; distinguish a failed full run from a passing isolated rerun.
- Integration tests must clean up every external resource they create, including RabbitMQ **exchanges and queues** (plus bindings, consumers, channels, and connections). Cleanup must run on success, failure, exception, cancellation, timeout, and partial initialization (`try/finally`, fixture teardown, or `await using`).
- Every test-created broker resource must have an unambiguous owner (typically `RabbitMqBrokerLab` / `BrokerLabContext` with unique `nms.m8.*` names). One test must never delete another test's or production resources; never purge a vhost or use blanket topology deletion.
- Broker-backed tests run only against the disposable test broker from `deploy/test-broker/Start-TestBroker.ps1` (container on `127.0.0.1`, dedicated vhost, random per-run credentials). `RabbitMqBrokerLab` reads its connection settings only from the gitignored `.artifacts/test-broker/broker.json` that script writes — never from application `appsettings*.json`, production/lab brokers, or hardcoded values. Never print or log those credentials.
- Broker-backed tests use `[SkippableFact]` / `[SkippableTheory]` and call `RabbitMqBrokerLab.RequireBroker()` (or `CreateIsolatedContext`): a missing broker fails them; they skip only when `NMS_TEST_BROKER_REQUIRED=false` is set explicitly (Windows CI, which cannot run Linux containers). Never turn broker connection failures into skips or passes. A plain `[Fact]` reports a skip as a failure, so the skippable attributes are needed for that explicit opt-out.
- Historical test leaks (`nms.m4.q|ex.<12-hex>` from the old integration tests) are remediated automatically by `RabbitMqLabFixture` / `RemediateHistoricalIntegrationTestLeaksAsync` before lab topology is created. That pass is idempotent, ownership-scoped only, and uses the same test-broker settings as AMQP tests — management HTTP Basic auth reuses those credentials; do not add a second credential source. A remediation failure is recorded on the fixture (asserted by `HistoricalLeakRemediationTests`), not thrown, so broker-independent tests in the collection are unaffected.
- Process-wide measurements (`GC.GetTotalMemory`, working set) are only meaningful with no concurrent tests: place them in a `DisableParallelization` collection (see `ProcessHeapMeasurementCollection`).
- Pipeline replay tests must not treat `DatagramBufferPool.RentedCount == 0` as "all records delivered": aggregation shards hand off asynchronously and `StopAsync` abandons queued Applies. Also wait for consumer emissions to reach `PipelineTestFactory.TotalRoutedApplies`.
- After a broker-backed test run, the broker must not retain resources created by that run. Verify against the real broker when changing cleanup.
